When doing backend security review or writing backend code in this repo, use the
skill defined in `SKILL.md` at the repo root as the source of truth. Read
`SKILL.md` first for the router and mode logic, then open the relevant
`references/*.md` file(s). At review-time read `01-audit-workflow.md` before
any topic file: it owns the sweep — the phase order and what each phase hands
the next, the entry-point inventory
that has to be enumerated from declarations rather than from a generated
schema, the principals and boundaries, hypothesis ordering, the budget rule
that enumerates a tree too large to read closely in full and rations only the
close reading, the six-item gate
a hypothesis discharges before it is written as a finding together with the
benign Django and DRF patterns it rules out, the coverage
ledger that keeps examined-and-clean apart from not-examined and is read back
at each phase boundary rather than recalled, and the WSTG
mapping at section granularity naming which testing-guide sections the sweep
covers and which are declared non-goals — and the topic
files answer the questions that sweep raises. The router is grouped — OWASP
spine, cross-cutting
surfaces, package decisions — and the "Ownership and boundaries" section below
it names the single owning file whenever two rows could both match, as a table
of topic, owner, and deciding distinction with three splits kept in prose, so
read that before assuming a topic is duplicated.
Review-time produces prioritized findings (severity,
location, CWE + OWASP mapping, an optional ASVS 5.0 chapter or WSTG section
where the project is held to that standard, the confirmed source-to-sink path
and the protection
that failed, fix); write-time applies the secure-default
contract and closes with a short security-decisions note rather than a findings
report. Each write-time rule sits beside the control it completes in the
reference file, so the file you opened for the concern already carries the rule
for writing it. The methodology, both output formats, the severity rubric
including how a race and a surviving-personal-data failure are rated, the
baseline severity table beneath it that makes an ordinary finding class
reproducible between runs, the ASVS
chapter mapping and its declared non-goals, and the rule for a secure default
that conflicts with the request are in
`references/00-methodology-and-severity.md`.

Treat the privilege model and object-/field-level authorization, impersonation
and break-glass access, OAuth2/OIDC and social login, API-key lifecycle/scoping,
agent and MCP tool surfaces, and third-party security-library vetting as
first-class review triggers. Use `agent-and-llm-interfaces.md` whenever a
backend endpoint is exposed to an agent or an LLM-driven caller. Use A01
for per-request access-control failures, for SSRF wherever a server-side
fetch takes its URL from input, and for path traversal wherever `open()`,
`os.path.join`, or a `pathlib` join takes any part of its path from a request,
and `authorization-architecture.md` for
the model behind them, `privileged-access-and-impersonation.md` for operator
privilege, A07 for auth/API keys and whenever `AUTH_PASSWORD_VALIDATORS`, a
password validator, or a passkey/WebAuthn setting is in view, A02 whenever
`set_signed_cookie` or `get_signed_cookie` is in view, A03 for the dependency
gate and whenever a
requirements file, extras group, Dockerfile install layer, or a CI workflow
that installs, scans, generates an SBOM, or attests a build artifact is in
view — judge each of those by what its exit code does rather than by whether
the step is present, treat an SBOM as an inventory rather than as integrity
evidence, and never write down a verification step that omits the expected
signer identity and issuer or a SLSA level above the one the platform's own
documentation supports, which for artifact attestations alone is Build
Level 2,
`data-layer-and-database.md` whenever database roles, row-level security, tenant
context on pooled connections, encrypted columns, raw SQL, NoSQL queries, read
replicas, a raised transaction isolation level, or connection pooling are in
view, `data-lifecycle-and-privacy.md` whenever deletion, soft delete,
retention, anonymization, personal-data exports,
or copies of a record in indexes, caches, files, or lower environments are in
view, `service-identity-and-secrets.md` whenever service-to-service calls,
machine tokens, JWKS, client credentials, mutual TLS, workload identity,
internal-only endpoints, secret storage or delivery, or `SECRET_KEY` rotation
are in view, `graphql-and-alternative-api-surfaces.md` whenever a GraphQL
schema, resolver, mutation, a non-DRF framework such as Django Ninja, or a
gRPC servicer or `.proto` service definition is in view — on that last one,
record that a server with no interceptor serves every registered method to
any caller, that authenticating a caller has decided nothing about which
methods or objects they may reach, and that `max_send_message_length` and
`maximum_concurrent_rpcs` have no default at all while the receive cap is
4 MB, `api-drf-specific.md` whenever a viewset, custom `@action`, serializer,
filter backend, paginator, throttle class, OpenAPI schema route, API version,
or bulk endpoint is in view, `file-uploads.md` whenever a `FileField`, an
upload endpoint, a `STORAGES` setting, an object-store or bucket
configuration, a presigned or signed URL, a direct-to-storage upload, or a
private download or media-serving path is in view,
`a06-insecure-design.md` whenever a model field holding a balance, price,
quantity, currency, credit, plan, tier, seat, expiry, or status is written from
anywhere — a view, a management command, a Celery task, an admin action, a
signal receiver, a data migration, or a bulk queryset write — or whenever a
checkout, refund, coupon, referral, invitation, entitlement grant, trial, or
export flow is in view, `a10-exceptional-conditions.md` whenever a
transaction, a lock, a database constraint, an idempotency key, a retried or
duplicated request, a state transition, a background-task dispatch, or an
error/exception handler on a security path is in view,
`a08-integrity-and-deserialization.md` whenever an inbound or outbound webhook,
an HMAC or signature check, a Celery task or broker message, a cache or session
serializer, a fixture load, or any call that turns bytes back into objects is in
view, `a04-cryptographic-failures.md` whenever a password hasher, a
`PASSWORD_HASHERS` setting, a change of hasher family or a wrapped-hasher
migration, a generated token or random value, a secret comparison, a
`Signer`/`TimestampSigner` salt, an encryption key or its rotation, a
`generate_data_key` or KMS wrap/unwrap call, or an encrypted field's key
material is in view,
`a02-security-misconfiguration.md` whenever a settings module, a CORS or CSP
policy, or a DNS record carrying SPF, DKIM, DMARC, or CAA is in view,
`deployment-and-runtime.md` whenever a Dockerfile, a `.dockerignore`, a proxy
or ingress configuration, a TLS group or cipher list such as `ssl_ecdh_curve`,
an `X-Forwarded-*` header, a client-IP lookup, a
Gunicorn or systemd unit, or a debug, profiling, metrics, or health endpoint is
in view, `a05-injection.md` whenever raw SQL, a user-supplied column or sort
key, a GeoDjango spatial or raster lookup, a `subprocess` or `os.system` call,
`mark_safe` or a template built from a
string, an LDAP filter, or a response or mail header built from input is in
view — it carries the inventory of every sink and the reference that owns each
one — and the security-hardening library index for dated package
dispositions.

Read-only triage scripts are in `scripts/`; all three parse with the `ast`
module rather than grepping lines, so a hit is a structural match carrying the
reference file that owns it, `entrypoint_inventory.py` enumerates the declared
entry points a sweep starts from, and `settings_scan.py` reads a settings
package rather than a single module. Do not rely on this summary alone — read
the referenced files.
